---
title: Branching
order: 2
description: Branches, switching, merging, and rebasing
tags: [git, branch, checkout, switch, merge, rebase]
---

Branches are lightweight pointers to commits. Use them freely for features,
fixes, and experiments.

@code bash
# List branches
git branch                       # local branches
git branch -r                    # remote-tracking branches
git branch -a                    # all branches
git branch -v                    # show last commit on each branch
git branch --merged              # branches merged into current
git branch --no-merged           # branches not yet merged

# Create branches
git branch feature-auth          # create branch (stay on current)
git branch feature-auth abc1234  # branch from specific commit

# Delete branches
git branch -d feature-auth       # delete if merged
git branch -D feature-auth       # force delete
git push origin --delete feature-auth   # delete remote branch

# Rename branches
git branch -m old-name new-name
git branch -m new-name           # rename current branch

@text
Use `git switch` (modern) or `git checkout` to change branches.

@code bash
# Switch branches
git switch main
git switch -c new-feature        # create and switch
git switch -c fix-123 origin/main  # branch from remote

# Checkout (older, still works)
git checkout main
git checkout -b new-feature      # create and switch
git checkout -- file.go          # discard working tree changes (dangerous)

# Detached HEAD
git switch --detach v2.0         # checkout a tag
git checkout abc1234             # checkout a commit

@text
Merging combines branch histories. Fast-forward merges move the pointer;
three-way merges create a merge commit.

@code bash
# Merge a branch into current
git merge feature-auth
git merge --no-ff feature-auth   # force merge commit (no fast-forward)
git merge --squash feature-auth  # squash all commits into staging area
git merge --abort                # cancel merge in progress

# Resolve conflicts
# 1. Edit conflicted files (look for <<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>>)
# 2. Stage resolved files
git add resolved-file.go
# 3. Complete the merge
git commit

@text
Rebasing replays commits onto a new base. Produces linear history but
rewrites commits -- never rebase published/shared branches.

@code bash
# Rebase current branch onto main
git rebase main
git rebase main feature-auth     # rebase feature-auth onto main

# Interactive rebase (edit, squash, reorder commits)
git rebase -i HEAD~5             # last 5 commits
git rebase -i main               # all commits since diverging from main
# In editor: pick, reword, edit, squash, fixup, drop

# Continue after resolving conflicts
git rebase --continue
git rebase --abort               # cancel rebase
git rebase --skip                # skip current commit

# Rebase with autosquash (use fixup!/squash! commit prefixes)
git commit --fixup=abc1234
git rebase -i --autosquash main

# Pull with rebase instead of merge
git pull --rebase
git config --global pull.rebase true   # make it default
